  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 1225 AM CDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nWith the ridge building west that will bring drier conditions to \nportions of the southeast to possibly central Panhandles and the \nwestern Panhandles will still have some moisture available for \nstorms on Wednesday.  This will be mainly north and west of \nan Amarillo to Canadian line. \n\nThursday through Sunday will be interesting as far as rain chances. \nThe ridge of high pressure is still tracking further west, as a \nclosed low undercuts the ridge over FL and into the Gulf (possible \ntropical system development down the line).  At the same time the \nas this low tracks west toward the northeast Texas coast, another \nupper trough will swing in from the Pacific Northwest and start to \nreally squeeze off this ridge and dissolve it out.  This will bring \nincrease chances of showers and storms to the Panhandles for the \nmajority of the extended, but the extent of where and how much \nrainfall will occur is still highly in question.  But it sure looks \nlike a pattern chance to near normal temperatures and above normal \nprecipitation is on the way to the Panhandles for the extended. \n\nWeber",
